        Root cause:
        In `OrderedScheduler`'s `TimedRunnable.safeRun()`, the code called `taskPendingStats.registerSuccessfulEvent(initNanos, ...)`, passing the raw initialization timestamp instead of the elapsed time since task creation (should have been `MathUtils.elapsedNanos(initNanos)`). This caused the "task queued" duration stat to record a nonsensical/incorrect value (essentially the raw nanoTime rather than a duration). Additionally, the `submitOrdered(long/int orderingKey, SafeRunnable r)` methods executed the raw `SafeRunnable` directly via `chooseThread(orderingKey).execute(r)` instead of wrapping it with `timedRunnable(r)`, meaning task execution time and queued time were never tracked at all for tasks submitted through these methods — silently bypassing the stats instrumentation entirely. Together these two defects meant task execution/queueing stats were either wrong or not collected, which is the core bug being fixed (and which the new `enableTaskExecutionStats` config/test harness changes were built around verifying).

        Commit message:
        ISSUE #931,#907: Add option to track task execution time

Fixes a bug in OrderedScheduler which failed to track execution
time with some calls and adds an option to enable it in the bookie.  Also
fixes a bug with task_queued duration.

Add a simple mock for remembering stats long enough to verify that
counters are actually used and sensible in unit tests and bake it into
BookKeeperClusterTestCase so that we can write tests to ensure that the
stats are actually counted and make sense.  Use said mock to add simple
tests for top level read and write stats validating this fix.

